Why this is a separate file rather than a paragraph at the end of LICENSE

It used to be that paragraph. GitHub's licence detector matches LICENSE against
the canonical MIT text and returns NOASSERTION when anything follows it, so the
API reported this project as unlicensed — while the file, read by a human,
granted exactly MIT and always had.

That mattered more than cosmetics. This repository is the seed of the Wish It
Better network, whose second criterion is "a LICENSE with a real SPDX
identifier", checked against the licence API. The one project the network holds
up as its example was failing that criterion, in a way no reading of the text
would ever reveal — only a machine read would. KUNAI-360 had already hit the
same wall and solved it with two files (LICENSE and LICENSE-DESIGN); this is
that fix, applied to the project that wrote the rule.

The lesson generalises past this repo: a licence is consumed by detectors, not
only by readers, and a clarification appended in good faith can silently revoke
the machine-visible grant. Put the clarification beside the licence, never
inside it.
